Encasulation
Encapsulation is about hiding internal data and exposing controlled access through method or properties
Goal is to protect object state and maintain invariants

1. Data Protection
2. Maintainability
3. Loose Coupling
4. Enforcing Business rules

Encapsulation means hiding the internal data state and exposing the controlled access through public methods or properties, ensuring data
integrity and loose coupling.

It applies at multiple architecture level
- Class Level
- Module Level
- Microservice Level
- API Boundaries

Service hides database schema
Client only use API

Encapsulation is the principle of bundling data and methods that operate on that data within a class while restricting direct access to
the internal state. This is typcially achieved using access modifiers like private and exposing controlled access through method or 
properties. The goal is to protect the data integrity, enforce business rules and reduce coupling between components

Encapsulation ensures that all state changes go through controlled methods, which allow us to enforce invariants and apply concurrency 
control mechanism to protect them.

Domain objects should live in the Domain layer and should not depend on application or infrastructure layers.
Application services orchestrate domain entities, while infrastructure provides implementations like repositories.

Encapsulation helps by hiding internal implementation details behind service interfaces or APIs. 
Clients interact with the service through well-defined contracts while the internal data models, database schema, and business logic remain private.
This reduces coupling, allows services to evolve independently, and ensures that domain invariants are enforced through controlled operations.

Public fields allow unrestricted access to an object’s internal state. 
This bypasses validation logic and can violate domain invariants. 
Encapsulation solves this by keeping fields private and exposing controlled methods that enforce business rules and maintain data integrity.

Abstraction
Abstraction is the process of hiding implementation complexity and exposing only the essential behavior of an object through well defined
interfaces.
It allows developers to interact with a system at a high level without worrying about internal complexity, 
which reduces coupling and improves maintainability.
"User knows what the system does, not how it does it"

- Reduces Complexity
- Loose Coupling
- Replace implementation easily

Abstraction vs Encapsulation
Encapsulation is about hiding internal state and protecting the data, and exposing controlled access via method or properties
Abstraction is about hiding the implementation complexity and exposing the essential behavior of the object
Abstraction defines what an object can do, while encapsulation defines how its internal state is protected.

We should avoid abstraction when there is only a single implementation and no foreseeable variation. 
Premature abstraction can introduce unnecessary complexity and make the system harder to understand.

An interface defines a contract that specifies what behavior a class must implement, without providing implementation details. 
An abstract class can provide both abstract methods and shared implementation, making it useful when multiple classes share common behavior.

What “protect change points” means

When designing libraries or frameworks, abstraction should hide parts of the system that may change frequently.
Abstraction isolates parts of the system that change.So when the implementation changes:consumers DO NOT change

Inheritance
Inheritance is an object-oriented principle where a class derives from another class and inherits its properties and behavior. 
It allows code reuse and enables hierarchical relationships between classes. 
Inheritance is typically used to model “is-a” relationships and also enables polymorphism through method overriding.

While inheritance enables reuse, modern design often prefers composition over inheritance to reduce tight coupling and increase flexibility.

Inheritance can break encapsulation because subclasses gain access to internal behavior and may rely on implementation
details of the base class. If the base class changes internally, subclasses that depend on those details may break.
Type of inheritance
- Single Inheritance
- Multilevel Inheritance
- Hierarchal Inheritance
- Mutliple Inheritance (via Interfaces)

Polymorphism
Polymorphism is a ability of a object to take multiple forms, where the same interface or method can exhibit different behavior depending
upon the underlying object.
It is typically achieved through method overloading at compile time and method overriding at runtime, 
enabling flexible and extensible system design.

Runtime Polymorphism - Method Overriding - Interface + virtual/override
Compiletime Polymorphism - Method Overloading

Virtual dispatch works using a virtual method table (VTable). 
Each class with virtual methods maintains a table containing pointers to the method implementations. 
When a virtual method is called, the CLR looks up the actual object type at runtime and resolves the method from the VTable,
ensuring the correct overridden implementation is executed.

Inheritance allows a class to derive behavior from another class, representing an “is-a” relationship, 
while composition involves building classes using other objects, representing a “has-a” relationship. 
Inheritance can lead to tight coupling and fragile hierarchies, whereas composition promotes loose coupling and flexibility. 
Modern systems typically favor composition over inheritance for better maintainability and extensibility.

Dependency Injection is a design pattern where a class receives its dependencies from an external container rather than creating them itself. 
In ASP.NET Core, services are registered in the DI container in Program.cs. 
When a class is instantiated, the container automatically resolves and injects the required dependencies through the constructor. 
This promotes loose coupling, better testability, and easier maintenance.
